# Board review for use by several people at once: findings

Reviewed on 2026-11-21 against a running board built from branch
`fix/board-multi-user-review`, started with `workbook serve --addr
127.0.0.1:7391` in a throwaway repository with a local bare `origin`. Hostile
input went in through the board's own web API, the same requests a signed-in
person's browser or script can send. A second reader was played by headless
Google Chrome (driven over the DevTools protocol), which loaded every page, ran
the board's script, and reported any script that ran, any request to a planted
address, any dialog, any content security policy violation, and any download.
Nothing was run against this project's own tasks.

Summary:

- Fixed here: the user name and password in `origin`'s address reached the
  browser of whoever changed a status while publishing failed (item 6). A file
  name, a link's label or address, the project's name in a tab title, a task's
  Title field, or any other text using a right-to-left override could make an
  `.exe` read as a `.pdf` on the board, including when a markdown reference gave
  the file a name of its own, and the download header carried the override too
  (item 2). The page's policy now limits form submissions to the board (item 1).
- Proposed as follow-up tasks: a bound on what one person can write and have in
  flight (item 4), an honest record of who edited a comment (item 5), and
  keeping operational detail out of a hosted board's answers (item 6).
- Found sound: script injection through any text field, attachments opened in
  the browser, links in task text, and reflected address parameters.

## 1. One person's text in another person's browser

**Tried.** Planted markup and script in every field the API accepts:

- Payloads used: `"'><img src=/pwned/T id=wbpwn-T onerror=...>`, `<svg
  onload=...>`, `<script>...</script>`, `</style></title></textarea>`, an
  `<iframe srcdoc>`, `{{7*7}}`, `${7*7}`, and an `<a href="javascript:...">`.
  Short forms of the same were used where a field has a small ceiling.
- Task title, description, labels, comment body, assignee, attachment file
  name, link label, status label, priority label, and the project's display
  name: all accepted the payloads and stored them.
- Status, priority and key names: refused at the door. Status and priority
  names must be lowercase tokens of at most 40 bytes, and keys must match
  `^[A-Z][A-Z0-9]{1,9}$`. A priority color must be `#rrggbb`. So
  `red;}</style><b>` is refused with "color ... must be six hexadecimal digits".
- Description markdown: headings, bold, quotes, lists and code blocks holding
  the payload. Links to `javascript:`, `JaVaScRiPt:`, `java\tscript:`,
  `&#106;avascript:`, `data:text/html,...`, `vbscript:`, a relative `/api/tasks`
  and a protocol-relative `//evil.example`. Images from `https://example.com`
  and from a `data:image/svg+xml` URI. Raw `<img onerror>`.
- A second task whose title is `<b id=wbpwn-t2>second</b>`, which made it a
  prerequisite of the first, so hostile text also appeared in the other task's
  dependency list.
- Address parameters reflected into the page: `/?q=&label=&priority=&key=`,
  `/tasks/new?status=`, `/tasks/<payload>`, `/config?x=#...`, and `/<payload>`.

**What happened.** Chrome loaded `/`, `/?deleted=1`, both task pages, `/config`
and `/tasks/new`, with descriptions shown on cards. On every one:

- `window.__pwn` was never set.
- No element with a planted id existed.
- There was exactly one `<script>` (the board's own) and no `on*` attribute
  anywhere.
- There were no frames.
- No anchor had a `javascript:`, `data:` or `vbscript:` address.
- No request went to a `/pwned/` address.
- No dialog opened and no exception was thrown.

Every payload was visible on the page as literal text, for example the text
`<script>__pwn=2</script>` in the header and in the `<title>`. Markdown links
other than absolute `http`/`https` were drawn as text, and external images were
drawn as text. The style blocks the server composes contained only the
validated priority tokens. The reflected parameters behaved the same way: no
script ran, and an unknown path got Go's plain `404 page not found`.

The code agrees with the browser. The server page is `html/template`. The two
`template.CSS` blocks are composed from validated tokens and re-rendered hex
colors. The client script builds every node with `createElement` and
`textContent`; the existing `TestHandlerClientScriptHasNoHTMLSink` guards
against `innerHTML`, and the markdown renderer allows only http/https links and
images that are attachments of the task.

**Finding: sound.** Nothing a signed-in person types runs or acts in another
person's browser. One residual risk is worth knowing: the page's policy allows
`'unsafe-inline'` script, so the policy would not catch a future slip into an
HTML sink. The guard test above is what stands there today.

The page's policy had no `form-action`, which is one of the directives that
does not fall back to `default-src`, so a form planted in the page could have
posted what a reader typed anywhere. It now carries `form-action 'self'`
(commit "Keep the board's forms posting to the board"). In headless Chrome the
board, a task page, the new-task page, the configuration page and a compacted
task's page loaded at 1440x900, 1280x800 and 390x844 with no policy violation,
a comment posted through the task page's own form, and a task was created
through the new-task form.

## 2. One person's attachment in another person's browser

**Tried.** Uploaded files through `POST /api/tasks/<id>/attachments`:

- `evil.html`, with script that reads `/api/tasks` and touches `window.opener`.
- `evil.svg`, with `onload` and a `<script>`.
- `evil.pdf`, with an `OpenAction` JavaScript.
- `evil.js`.
- `html-as.png` (HTML bytes under a `.png` name), and a real 1x1 `real.png`.
- `quote"; filename="pwn.html`, to try to inject the `Content-Disposition`
  header.
- `crlf\r\nX-Injected: 1.txt`, to try to inject a response header.
- `../../../../etc/passwd`.
- `rtl<U+202E>gnp.exe`.
- A name containing `<img onerror>`.

Also sent the API-only `media` member with `text/html`, `image/svg+xml`,
`image/png`, `image/png; charset=utf-8`, `IMAGE/PNG` and `text/html,
image/png`. Then opened every attachment directly in Chrome. Also referenced
attachments from the description as `![..](attachment:<id>)`, and tried to
frame one from the board page.

**What happened.**

- Path names were refused ("attached file name must not contain a path
  separator"). An SVG media type was refused ("an SVG served inline can carry
  script"). Malformed media types were refused.
- `text/html` was stored but served as `application/octet-stream`. HTML, SVG,
  PDF, JS and every other non-image type were answered
  `Content-Disposition: attachment`, `Content-Type: application/octet-stream`,
  `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` and `Content-Security-Policy: default-src
  'none'; sandbox`. Chrome downloaded each one, which the DevTools
  `downloadWillBegin` event recorded. The tab stayed on `about:blank`, no
  script ran, and no request was made.
- The quote name was served as `filename="quote\"; filename=\"pwn.html"` and
  saved as `quote_; filename=_pwn.html`. The CRLF name was served as
  `filename*=utf-8''crlf%0D%0AX-Injected%3A%201.txt`, so no header was injected.
- `html-as.png` and `real.png` were served inline as `image/png` with nosniff
  and the sandbox policy. Opened in a tab, Chrome showed an image or a broken
  image, and no script ran. Referenced from the description, both became
  `<img>`: the real one decoded, the HTML one did not. SVG and HTML references
  became download links. Framing an attachment from the board page was blocked
  by the page's own `default-src 'none'`.
- **Defect:** the board drew `rtl<U+202E>gnp.exe` as **rtlexe.png** in Chrome
  (screenshot taken), while the file downloads as an `.exe` (Chrome saved it as
  `rtl_gnp.exe`). The command line already prints names through
  `core.DisplayLine`, which neutralizes exactly this spoof; the board did not.
  The same name was drawn raw in a description's reference to the file and in
  the history.

**Finding: fixed here** (commits "Draw attachment names on the board without
direction overrides", "Draw typed text on the board without direction
controls" and "Refuse direction controls in a new link attachment's address").

The first fix cleaned a file's name only where a markdown reference gave no
text of its own. A re-review showed two bypasses:

- `![invoice.pdf](attachment:<id>)`, where the file is really named
  `invoice<U+202E>fdp.exe`, drew a link reading "invoice.pdf" that downloads as
  `invoice_fdp.exe`.
- A link attachment to `https://evil.example/<U+202E>fdp.exe` was accepted, and
  its row and tooltip read `https://evil.example/exe.pdf`.

Now:

- A markdown reference to a file that is not drawn as an image always reads as
  the file's cleaned real name. The author's text follows it as a caption in
  parentheses, cleaned the same way.
- A link attachment's label, address and tooltip are drawn with control
  characters and direction overrides as spaces, as a file's name is.
- A new link whose address holds a control or bidi-control character is refused
  ("attachment URL must not contain control or direction-control characters"),
  on the board and the command line. Stored ones still display, cleaned.
- Everywhere else the board draws text somebody typed, the direction
  overrides, embeddings and isolates (U+202A to U+202E, U+2066 to U+2069)
  become spaces: titles, descriptions, comments, labels, assignees, status and
  priority labels, the project's name, history, tooltips, accessible names, an
  image's `alt`, and every route's tab title. The project's name and the tab
  title suffix are cleaned where the script reads them, so `/config`,
  `/tasks/new`, "Task not found" and the board itself are titled cleanly on load
  and after a navigation inside the page.
- The three directional marks (U+200E, U+200F, U+061C) are kept on the board.
  A second review found that replacing them moved the punctuation of honest
  Hebrew and Arabic text, and a mark cannot reverse a run of letters. The
  command line's `core.DisplayLine` still replaces them; the reference says the
  two differ and why.
- A task's Title field and the configuration page's project name, status label
  and priority label fields hold the cleaned text. A save treats a field that
  still reads exactly that as unchanged and sends the stored text (or nothing),
  so saving another field never rewrites the stored title or name. The
  description and comment editors, which open only on Edit, show stored text
  exactly.
- The download route sends a file's name with each control character and each
  override, embedding or isolate as `_`, in the plain and the RFC 2231 form, so
  the server no longer relies on the browser to clean it. Stored names are
  unchanged.
- A drawn file name (row, reference, history) reads the blank-looking fillers
  U+3164, U+115F, U+1160, U+FFA0, U+2800, U+180E and every space separator as a
  space and collapses each run to one, so padding cannot push `.exe` out of
  sight.
- An attachment image's `alt` is the file's cleaned real name, with the
  author's text after it in parentheses, the rule a file reference follows.

