`TestUpdateRefusesBlankAndMissingThreadTargets`, and, from the second review,
`TestClientTabTitlesDrawTheProjectNameWithoutDirectionOverrides`,
`TestClientTaskTitleFieldShowsTheCleanTitleAndASaveLeavesTheStoredOneAlone`,
`TestClientConfigFieldsShowCleanTextAndSaveTheStoredText`,
`TestAttachmentDownloadSendsAFileNameWithoutDirectionControls`,
`TestAttachmentDownloadFormatsAHostileFileName`,
`TestBoardKeepsDirectionalMarksAndReplacesOverrides`,
`TestHandlerClientDrawsFileNamesWithoutBlankFillers`,
`TestHandlerClientNamesAnAttachmentImageByItsFile`,
`TestHandlerClientDrawsImagesOnlyAsAttachmentsOfTheTask`,
`TestHandlerClientRedrawsACardsAccessibleNameWithoutDirectionOverrides` and
`TestAttachmentShownNameCleansALinksLabel`. The rest of this item is sound: an
opened attachment cannot reach the board or the reader's session.

## 3. Links

**Tried.** An `http://localhost:7392/...` link in a description (a different
origin from the board), link attachments, and links in comments. A small server
on 7392 logged each request's `Referer`, `Origin` and `Cookie`. Its landing page
reported `window.opener`, tried `window.opener.location`, `document.referrer`,
and tried to `fetch` the board's `/api/tasks`. The link was clicked with real
mouse events in Chrome.

**What happened.**

- Every outbound link the board draws has `target="_blank"`. Description,
  comment and link-attachment links have `rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow"`;
  file links have `rel="noopener noreferrer"`.
- The landing request arrived with no `Referer`, no `Origin` and no `Cookie`.
  On the landing page, `window.opener` was `null` and `document.referrer` was
  empty.
- Its `fetch` of the board was blocked (`TypeError`), because the board sends
  no CORS headers and refuses foreign `Origin`s.
- `javascript:`, `data:` and relative links were never drawn as links (item 1),
  and a link attachment with `javascript:alert(1)` was refused at the API
  ("attachment URL must use http or https").

**Finding: sound.** A link can take a reader anywhere on the web, but it
carries nothing from the board: no address, no opener, no session. The only
board data in such a request is what its author typed into the URL. The board
sets no `Referrer-Policy` header and relies on `rel` on each link; adding the
header would be optional hardening, not a fix.

## 4. Limits

**Tried.** One byte over the file ceiling. A body over the upload envelope. A
1 MiB body to an ordinary route. Filling one task with 1 MiB files. Adding and
removing a 1 MiB file 25 times on another task. Creating 60 tasks in a row.
Then floods, while a second client polled every 200 ms: 300 concurrent task
creates, 150 concurrent 1 MiB uploads, and 300 concurrent reads each of
history, attachment download, the board page and `/api/tasks`. Server memory
and child processes were sampled every 100 ms.

**What happened.**

- The ceilings hold as documented:
  - A file one byte over: "attached file big.bin is 1048577 bytes and must not
    exceed 1048576".
  - A body over the upload envelope: "request body must not exceed 1463640
    bytes".
  - An ordinary route: "request body must not exceed 1048576 bytes".
  - The eleventh 1 MiB file on one task: "task would hold 11534336 bytes of
    attached files and must not exceed 10485760".
  - 50 attachments and 500 comments per task, as documented.
- **Disk is not bounded.** The 25 add-and-remove rounds left no live
  attachment, yet the repository's `.git` grew by 26,536 KiB. Shared history is
  append-only, so removal frees nothing. There is no ceiling on tasks: all 60
  creates succeeded, as did the 300 in the flood. So one person can grow the
  server's disk, `origin`, and every teammate's clone (which fetches task refs)
  by about 10 MiB per task, as fast as they can send requests.
- **One person can stall everyone.** At rest, the second client's poll took
  34–55 ms and the server used 56 MB.
  - 300 concurrent creates: 181 concurrent Git processes. The other reader's
    poll took up to 11,104 ms (median 708 ms). All 300 succeeded in 14.3 s.
  - 150 concurrent uploads: server memory 608 MB, 144 Git processes, the other
    reader's poll up to 4,902 ms.
  - Read floods: up to 109 Git processes and 700 MB of memory, other readers
    up to 1.4 s.
  - Nothing failed, but nothing bounds it either: ten times the flood would
    reach the machine's process or memory limits.
- Slow or stalled connections are bounded already: 10 s header timeout, 30 s
  read timeout, 2 min idle.

**Finding: proposed as follow-up** (Follow-up A below). The per-request and
per-task ceilings hold. Bounding disk and in-flight work per person needs to
know which person sent a request, which "Run the board behind a sign-in proxy"
introduces, and a storage budget is a design decision. A single server-wide cap
on in-flight requests was considered and not built here: it would prevent a
crash but would still let one person fill the queue and stall everyone else,
which is what this item asks to prevent.

## 5. What a signed-in person can reach that a client should not

**Tried.** Every route and method:

- Listed the mux registrations in `internal/webui/handler.go`.
- Called each route.
- Sent wrong methods (`PUT /api/tasks` gets `405` with `Allow`) and unknown
  routes (`404`).
- Sent a foreign `Host` with curl (`403`, "Host "evil.example:7391" is not this
  board").
- Sent `text/plain` mutations (`415`).
- Exercised every write through the API: delete, restore, statuses, priorities,
  keys, display, sync mode, assignments, comments, attachments.
- Recorded a comment and an assignment as `bob@example.com` from the command
  line, then tried to change both through the board.

**What happened.** Every route behaves as documented, and every write is open
to everyone: there are no roles. Two results are worth calling out:

- The board **rewrote and then removed bob's comment.** After the edit the
  comment still read `"author":"bob@example.com"` with the new body and an
  `editedAt`. The history recorded the edit under the server's identity
  (`alice@example.com`), but the comment as drawn names bob. Core allows any
  identity to edit any comment, from the command line too, and on a hosted
  board every person writes as the server's identity. So one person can put
  words under a teammate's name, marked only "edited".
- Withdrawing bob's assignment was **refused**: "assignment bob@example.com ...
  may be removed only by bob@example.com". Core limits withdrawal to the
  assignee or whoever recorded the assignment.
- `PUT /api/sync` is a server-wide setting, so one person can switch everyone's
  publication mode.
- The page shows the serving checkout's `user.email` and the folder's last path
  element. History shows every recording identity's email.

The full list, by route, is in `board-review-capabilities.md` and is now in
`docs/reference.md` under "What anyone who can open the board can do and see".

**Finding: documented here, with one follow-up proposed** (Follow-up B: comment
edits under another person's name). Everything else on the list is a
capability, not a defect, because there are no roles. Writes being recorded
under the server's identity is what "Run the board behind a sign-in proxy"
addresses with the proxy-supplied name.

## 6. Error messages

**Tried.**

- Ordinary errors: unknown and malformed task IDs (including `..%2F..%2Fetc`
  and `%00`), malformed and over-long JSON, unknown members, bad base64, a
  missing attachment, bad expected heads, a bad sync mode, and 40 concurrent
  edits to one task.
- Publication failures, in inline mode, which any signed-in person can select:
  `origin` set to a missing path, to a `file://` that is not a repository, to
  `https://reviewer:s3cr3t-token@127.0.0.1:1/...`, to an `ssh://` URL, and to an
  HTTP server answering every request with a short line of plain text (not
  Git).
- What Git prints for credential URLs on 404, 401, 403, 500 and non-Git
  answers.

**What happened.**

- Ordinary errors are short and name only the request:
  - "no task matches "../../etc"", "decode task create", "attachment content
    must be standard base64", "task ... has no attachment ...".
  - "task ... has changed since zzzz; reload and try again".
  - The 40 concurrent edits: 1 × `200` and 39 × `409` "task ref changed
    concurrently". No lock-file paths.
- A failed publication of **any** change, a task's included, is answered
  `200` with an `auto-sync-incomplete` warning that carries the failing Git
  command, its exit status and Git's own stderr. An earlier version of this
  file said a failed task publication reads only "Git returned 0 push outcomes,
  want 1"; that is false. Re-run on 2026-11-21 against a board built from the
  branch, in inline mode, with `origin` at the unreachable
  `http://127.0.0.1:1/team/secret-path/repo.git`, `POST /api/tasks` and
  `POST /api/vocabulary/statuses` both answered:

  > the change was recorded locally, but publishing it failed: cannot ask
  > origin which Workbook project it holds before publishing: git ls-remote
  > --refs origin refs/workbook/project failed: exit status 128: fatal: unable
  > to access 'http://127.0.0.1:1/team/secret-path/repo.git/': Failed to
  > connect to 127.0.0.1 port 1 after 0 ms: Couldn't connect to server

  With `origin` at a missing folder, the same warning named the folder's full
  path on the server ("fatal: '/private/tmp/…/missing-remote.git' does not
  appear to be a git repository"). So a failed task publication reveals the
  Git command Workbook ran, `origin`'s full address including its path (with
  the credential removed), or the server's own path to `origin`.
- From the first review, a failed configuration publication carried the same
  kind of detail:
  - "could not publish refs/workbook/config to origin: fatal:
    '/tmp/wb-board-review.RdHYXa/missing-remote.git' does not appear to be a git
    repository"
  - "fatal: unable to access 'https://127.0.0.1:1/team/repo.git/': Failed to
    connect"
- **Defect, credential disclosure:** with `origin` at
  `http://reviewer:s3cr3t-token@127.0.0.1:7393/...` answering plain text,
  adding a status answered with this warning:

  > the change was recorded locally, but could not publish refs/workbook/config
  > to origin: XML error: syntax error; error: no DAV locking support on
  > http://reviewer:s3cr3t-token@127.0.0.1:7393/rmsg/repo.git/; fatal:
  > git-http-push failed

  Git anonymizes the URL in its 404/401/403/500 messages, but not in this one.
  Once that push failed, the sync watcher stopped answering ("no-watcher"), so
  every later change published inline too, even in deferred mode. Anyone who
  changed a status would have seen the token.

**Finding: fixed here** for the user name and password in a remote's address
(commits "Keep remote URL credentials out of Git failure reports" and "Remove
configured remote credentials from Git failures by their text").

The first fix matched a URL's userinfo with a pattern that stopped at a quote
or a slash. A re-review leaked:

- a password holding `'` or `"` through the board's warning;
- a password holding `/` through Git's own `fatal: unable to access '…'`, on
  the fetch path too, because Git's anonymizer gives up on a slash;
- the same passwords set through `pushurl` or `url.<base>.insteadOf`;
- the rest of a password holding an unencoded `@`.

When a Git command fails and its stderr holds an `@`, Workbook now reads the
addresses Git is configured with (`remote.*.url`, `remote.*.pushurl`,
`url.*.insteadOf`, `url.*.pushInsteadOf`, and the addresses those rewrites
produce). It removes each one's credential, by its exact text, from the
captured stderr and from the command an error names. A password of eight
characters or more is also removed on its own wherever it appears, as `***`.
A user name of that length is removed on its own only when its address has no
password, where it is usually a token. The second review found that a user
named `workbook` beside a password turned every failure into
`git ls-remote --refs origin refs/***/tasks/* …` and a path
`/workbook/repo.git` into `/***/repo.git`; beside a password the user name now
goes only inside its address. Re-run against a board built from the new head
with `origin` at `http://workbook:<password>@127.0.0.1:1/workbook/repo.git`:
the warning read `git ls-remote --refs origin refs/workbook/project failed: …
unable to access 'http://127.0.0.1:1/workbook/repo.git/'`. The pattern still removes any other `scheme://user@`, now
through the last `@`. A successful command costs no extra Git process. Object
batches redact the same way, since a partial clone's batch can fetch from its
promisor remote.

Re-run on a board built from the branch, against the same plain-text remote:

- With `'` set as the url, the pushurl, or the base of an insteadOf, the
  warning read "… no DAV locking support on
  http://127.0.0.1:7493/team/repo.git/ …".
- With `"`, `/` (fetch path) or `@`, `workbook sync --json` carried no part of
  the password and still named `http://127.0.0.1:7493/team/repo.git/`.

Tests:

- `TestRunServeKeepsOriginCredentialsOutOfPublishingWarnings` runs the real
