**Not removed**, and documented as such:

- Whatever a credential helper, a hook or the remote itself prints. A helper
  writing `refreshing token <token>` to its error output is reported as
  written.
- A token placed in an address's path or query string (`?access_token=…`).

The reference tells a deployment to keep its credential out of the address: a
credential helper that prints nothing, or an SSH key.

**Proposed as follow-up** for the rest (Follow-up C): on a hosted board,
operational errors and publication warnings still name the server's paths and
`origin`'s address, and carry Git's output. On a person's own machine that
detail is what makes a failure actionable, so it was kept as it is.

## For the hosting guide

- Until the hosting story (Follow-up C) changes it, a hosted board's failure
  answers still name the server's paths and `origin`'s address. A failed
  publication of any change, including creating or editing a task, is answered
  with a warning that carries the Git command Workbook ran and Git's own
  message, which names `origin`'s full address including its path, or the
  server's own path to `origin` when that is a folder. An operational error
  carries the same kind of detail.
- The user name and password in a remote's address are removed from those
  answers where Git prints them inside the address. Outside its address, a
  password of eight characters or more is removed, and so is a user name of
  that length when its address has no real password beside it (none, an empty
  one, one shorter than eight characters, or `x-oauth-basic`), since that user
  name is a token. Not removed: anything a credential helper, a Git hook or the remote
  itself prints, and a token placed in an address's path or query string
  (`https://git.example/repo.git?access_token=…`). Give the server its
  credential through a helper that prints nothing, or an SSH key, rather than in
  `origin`'s URL.
- Display limits that remain:
  - The board keeps U+200E, U+200F and U+061C. They cannot reverse a run of
    letters, so a file name's extension reads as stored (checked in Chrome:
    `invoice.exe`, `fdp.exe` and `exe.pdf` all drew in order). They can
    reorder digits and punctuation, invisibly: a title stored as
    `Amount <RLM>10<RLM>.<RLM>99 due` reads "Amount 99.10 due", `Pay 1,000
    now` can read "Pay 000,1 now", and `photo_2024-11.png` can read
    "photo11-2024_.png". An amount or a date that matters should be read from
    `workbook show`, which prints the marks as spaces.
  - The relationship picker's field holds a chosen task's title as stored,
    overrides included, once the task is picked; the list of options it
    offers is cleaned.
  - Blank-looking fillers are read as a space only in file names. A title,
    label or comment can still be padded with U+3164 or U+2800 to push its
    end out of view.
  - Characters that look alike (a Cyrillic `а` for a Latin `a`), and zero-width
    characters such as U+200B, are drawn as they are anywhere on the board.
  - The download header replaces controls and direction characters but not
    fillers, so a padded name is saved with its padding.
  - The `data-` attributes the script reads from (`data-project-name`,
    `data-title-suffix`, `data-status-label`) hold the stored text. Nothing
    draws them as they are, but a browser extension or a future script that
    did would draw the override.

## Proposed follow-up tasks

### A. Bound what one person can write and have in flight on a hosted board

One signed-in person can grow the server's disk, `origin`, and every clone
without limit, and can stall the board for everyone with concurrent requests.
Measured on 2026-11-21:

- 25 add-and-remove rounds of a 1 MiB attachment grew `.git` by 26.5 MB with
  nothing left live.
- 300 concurrent creates ran 181 Git processes at once and slowed another
  reader's poll from about 40 ms to 11 s.
- 150 concurrent uploads took the server from 56 MB to 608 MB.

Per-request and per-task ceilings already hold. What is missing is a per-person
budget, keyed by the name the sign-in proxy supplies:

- a cap on requests one person has in flight, with the excess answered `429`
  rather than queued behind everyone else;
- a budget of bytes written per person per window (attachments and text), with
  a clear refusal naming the window.

Decide whether there is also a server-wide ceiling on tasks or stored bytes. If
there is, remember that removal never frees space in append-only history;
recovery is deleting the tasks and `workbook compact --deleted-only`. Document
the defaults in the hosting guide, along with the proxy-side request rate and
connection limits a deployment should set meanwhile.

Depends on: "Run the board behind a sign-in proxy" (for the per-person name).

Does hosting have to wait? I believe not for a team that admits only people it
trusts not to vandalize, provided the hosting guide states the risk and tells
deployers to set per-user rate and connection limits at the proxy. Damage is
attributed once the proxy name lands, and it can be cleaned up with delete and
compact. For a team admitting people it does not trust that far, hosting should
wait on this.

### B. Show who edited a comment, or limit editing to its author

On a hosted board, every person can rewrite or remove any comment, and a
rewritten comment is still drawn under its original author with only an
"edited" mark. Seen live: bob's comment, rewritten through the board, read
`"author":"bob@example.com"` with the new body. The history records the editor,
but the thread does not show it. Core allows any identity to edit any comment
(the command line too); assignments, by contrast, are limited to their assignee
or whoever recorded them.

Two possible fixes:

- Record and draw the editor on the comment itself ("edited by sam@…").
- Apply the assignment rule to comments: author or nobody, with a removal
  allowed and recorded.

Either is a core semantics change that needs per-person identity on the board.

Depends on: "Run the board behind a sign-in proxy" (for the per-person name).

Does hosting have to wait? I believe yes. Words shown under a teammate's name
that the teammate did not write are the most direct way left for one person to
harm another through the board, and coding agents read comments as
instructions.

### C. Keep operational detail out of a hosted board's answers

A failed publication of any change, a task's included, answers with a
warning carrying the failing Git command and Git's stderr, and an operational
error carries the failing Git command and its output. Together
these name the server's folder (for example
`'/srv/…/missing-remote.git' does not appear to be a git repository`) and
`origin`'s address (`unable to access 'https://git.internal/team/repo.git/'`).
The user name and password in a remote's address are already removed (fixed in
this pull request); a credential helper's own output and a token in an
address's path or query are not.

When the board runs at a public URL:

- answer these with a short sentence ("publishing to origin failed; the server
  log has the details"), and
- write the full detail to `serve`'s stderr with a correlation id.

Keep today's full detail on a person's own board, where it is what makes the
failure actionable.

Depends on: "Run the board behind a sign-in proxy" (`--public-url` is the
switch).

Does hosting have to wait? No. The detail reveals infrastructure, not access,
and the hosting guide already lists it.
